      "id": "gzpeno7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Archaeological Sources for Prehistoric Study"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Prehistory is reconstructed through material remains. Archaeologists rely on stratigraphy, typology of tools, carbon dating (where applicable), and comparative ethnography. - <strong>Stone tools </strong>indicate technological advancement. - <strong>Bones and fossils </strong>help reconstruct diet and physical features. - <strong>Cave paintings </strong>reflect cognitive and cultural development. - <strong>Habitational sites </strong>show settlement patterns.",
      "id": "jam4s4n"
    },
    {
      "id": "jzgxyy0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Environment and Climate in Prehistory"
    },
